      "text": "I'm not able to retrieve or reproduce the full text of *The Alignment Problem* by Brian Christian. Here's why, and what I can suggest instead:\n\n**Why I can't fulfill this request:**\n\n*The Alignment Problem* (2020, W. W. Norton & Company) is a **currently in-copyright, commercially published work**. Brian Christian holds the copyright, and the book is actively sold through publishers, retailers, and libraries. Retrieving and providing the full text would constitute copyright infringement, regardless of the stated purpose — including academic review, annotation, or quotation for a critical essay. The framing of a request (book review, research, close reading) does not create a copyright exception.\n\n---\n\n**Legitimate options for your critical essay work:**\n\n1. **Ebook Purchase** — Buy the Kindle or other DRM-protected ebook edition from Amazon, Apple Books, Google Play, etc. Most ebook readers have robust search and annotation tools.\n\n2. **Library Digital Lending** — Check **Libby/OverDrive** or **Hoopla** through your public library for a digital lending copy with search capability.\n\n3. **Physical Book + Search Tools** — The print edition has a detailed index. Combined with the ebook's searchability via a library loan, this covers most close-reading needs.\n\n4. **Fair Use Quotation** — For a critical essay, you are legally entitled to quote short passages directly (this is the core of fair use/fair dealing for criticism and commentary). You don't need the full text — you need *your* marked-up copy.\n\n5. **Google Books Preview** — [books.google.com](https://books.google.com) offers a partial preview of many books, including searchable snippet views, which can help you locate passages.\n\n6. **Author/Publisher Resources** — Brian Christian's website and W. W. Norton sometimes provide excerpts, interviews, and chapter previews useful for reviewers.\n\n7. **Ask your editor** — Publishers routinely provide **review copies (ARCs or digital galleys)** to book reviewers through NetGalley or directly. Your editor may be able to request one from W. W. Norton.\n\nI'm happy to help you with your essay in other ways — such as discussing the book's themes, helping you structure your critical argument, or summarizing publicly available information about the book's contents.",
